= Solution
{parent=A1}

The <Stefan–Boltzmann law> gives the radiated <luminosity>
$$
L=4\pi R_\odot^2\sigma T_{\rm eff}^4\simeq3.69\times10^{26}\,\mathrm W.
$$
For <radiative mass loss at fixed luminosity>, using <mass-energy equivalence>, the mass-loss magnitude and one-percent time are
$$
\boxed{|\dot M|=L/c^2\simeq4.10\times10^9\,\mathrm{kg\,s^{-1}},\qquad t_{1\%}=\frac{0.01M_\odot c^2}{L}\simeq4.88\times10^{18}\,\mathrm s\simeq1.55\times10^{11}\,\mathrm{yr}.}
$$
The numerical constants are the external CODATA 2014 $\sigma=5.670367\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}K^{-4}}$ and $c=299792458\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$; years here mean 365.25 days.<CODATA recommended values of the fundamental physical constants 2014 by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Stefan-Boltzmann constant and vacuum light speed]{ref} This estimates radiative mass loss with the supplied radius and <temperature> held constant. It does not predict a real stellar lifetime or include solar-wind mass loss.

= A2
{parent=Section A}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=A2}

For this <molecular escape-speed temperature estimate>, $v_{\rm rms}^2=3k_BT/m$. Equating this to the surface escape-speed square $v_{\rm esc}^2=2GM_\oplus/R_\oplus$ gives
$$
\boxed{T=\frac{2mGM_\oplus}{3k_BR_\oplus}\simeq1.40\times10^5\,\mathrm K.}
$$
This evaluation keeps the paper's $R_\oplus=6.4\times10^6\,\mathrm m$ and $m=28u$, and uses externally documented $GM_\oplus=3.9860\times10^{14}\,\mathrm{m^3s^{-2}}$, $u=1.660539040\times10^{-27}\,\mathrm{kg}$ and $k_B=1.38064852\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$.<Earth Fact Sheet by National Aeronautics and Space Administration>[Bulk parameters: gravitational parameter GM]{ref}<CODATA recommended values of the fundamental physical constants 2014 by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Atomic mass constant and Boltzmann constant]{ref} The corresponding escape speed is about $11.2\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$.

This is the requested speed-scale comparison. Atmospheric escape can occur from the high-speed tail at lower <temperature>; molecular dissociation and ionization also prevent treating an actual gas at this <temperature> as unchanged neutral nitrogen molecules.

= A3
{parent=Section A}
{scope}
