= Solution
{parent=A7}

The first ideal linear polarizer transmits half of an unpolarized beam. Each subsequent polarizer is $60^\circ$ from the preceding transmitted <polarization>, so <Malus's law> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{I_{\rm out}}{I_{\rm in}}=\frac12\cos^2(60^\circ)\cos^2(60^\circ)=\frac1{32}.}
$$
Intensities, rather than coherent field amplitudes for different input <polarization> components, are averaged for the unpolarized beam. The output is polarized along the last polarizer's axis.

= A8
{parent=Section A}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=A8}

The <wave numbers> before and after the drop are $k=\sqrt{2mE}/\hbar$ and $q=\sqrt{2m(3E)}/\hbar=\sqrt3\,k$. Continuity of $\psi$ and $\psi'$ at the finite step gives
$$
1+r=t,\qquad k(1-r)=qt,\qquad r=\frac{k-q}{k+q}.
$$
Thus the reflected probability flux fraction is
$$
\boxed{R=|r|^2=\left(\frac{1-\sqrt3}{1+\sqrt3}\right)^2=7-4\sqrt3\simeq0.0718.}
$$
The transmission probability is $(q/k)|t|^2=1-R$, rather than simply $|t|^2$. Reflection is possible at a downward <potential step> even though both sides are classically allowed.

= A9
{parent=Section A}
