= Solution
{parent=A9}

For <hyperfine coupling for a spin-one nucleus>, let the <electron spin> be $s=1/2$ and the <nuclear spin> $I=1$. First couple $\mathbf j=\mathbf L+\mathbf s$, giving $j=L\pm1/2$, then $\mathbf F=\mathbf j+\mathbf I$, giving $F=|j-I|,|j-I|+1,\ldots,j+I$.

|| $L$
|| $j$
|| Allowed $F$

| $1$
| $1/2$
| $1/2,3/2$

| $1$
| $3/2$
| $1/2,3/2,5/2$

| $2$
| $3/2$
| $1/2,3/2,5/2$

| $2$
| $5/2$
| $3/2,5/2,7/2$

For each $F$, the projection is $m_F=-F,-F+1,\ldots,F$; electron projections similarly satisfy $m_j=-j,\ldots,j$. Summing $2F+1$ gives 18 states for $L=1$ and 30 for $L=2$, agreeing with $(2L+1)(2s+1)(2I+1)$.

**Notation qualification:** if the printed leading “2” denotes the principal <quantum number>, a hydrogenic $2d$ bound state does not exist because $L\leq n-1$; a physical $d$ state needs $n\geq3$. If a doublet term symbol $^{2}P$ or $^{2}D$ was intended, its leading 2 instead means $2s+1$, and the angular-<momentum> table applies without fixing $n$. The answers above use the explicitly specified $L$ values, while retaining this ambiguity.<Quantum Mechanics by David Tong>[Three-dimensional angular momentum and hydrogenic quantum-number restrictions]{ref}

= A10
{parent=Section A}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=A10}

The fastest clearly resolved late oscillations occupy roughly $0.004$–$0.005\,\mathrm s$ per cycle when compared with the $0.05\,\mathrm s$ major-tick spacing. Thus $f_{\max}$ is of order $200$–$250\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and the <Nyquist–Shannon sampling theorem> requires
$$
\boxed{f_s>2f_{\max}\sim400\text{–}500\,\mathrm{samples\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
A <sampling rate> of order $500\,\mathrm{samples\,s^{-1}}$ is the estimated lower boundary for the plotted signal bandwidth; practical acquisition needs anti-alias filtering and margin. This is not a determination of LIGO's actual acquisition rate.<What the Nyquist Criterion Means to Your Sampled Data System Design by Walt Kester>[MT-002: bandwidth-limited sampling and anti-alias filtering]{ref}

The <bit-depth requirements for waveform digitization> need a specified precision. **The numerical bit depth is underdetermined by the figure.** If the digitizer covers a full-scale range $V_{\rm FS}$ and the required quantization step is at most $\Delta$, then
$$
\boxed{b\geq\left\lceil\log_2\frac{V_{\rm FS}}{\Delta}\right\rceil.}
$$
For a uniform quantizer its ideal quantization-noise rms is approximately $\Delta/\sqrt{12}$. No permitted quantization error, noise criterion or calibrated digitizer range is specified. The plotted reconstruction band does not itself define one. Therefore no unique finite bit count, or exact lossless digitization of an arbitrary real-valued waveform, follows from the supplied data.

